s genuinely close, but the expert ratings consensus, Polymarket pricing, and Elijah's fresh primary mandate and local endorsements tilt the expected margin modestly toward Republicans despite Dalton's clear money advantage.", "watch": "Almost no recent public polling exists, so any credible general-election survey or a shift in the national environment could move this race in either direction.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Open seat: Cory Mills (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Florida's 7th District is an open seat after Republican incumbent Cory Mills lost renomination, leaving Republican Ryan Elijah to face Democrat Bale Dalton. The district carries a five-point Republican lean, but the national environment favors Democrats, and Dalton has outraised Elijah roughly four to one, with about $700,000 in outside support against none for Elijah. Expert ratings split between Likely Republican and Lean Republican, with two outlets calling it a Tossup and one calling a Democratic flip. Prediction markets give Republicans about a 65 percent chance. The only public poll is a Democratic-sponsored hypothetical from May, so the outlook rests mostly on fundamentals and ratings.\n\nThat mix points to a narrow Republican edge in a competitive race. Dalton's financial advantage and the favorable environment keep Democrats within striking distance, while the district's underlying lean and the ratings consensus tilt the expected outcome toward Elijah. The Libertarian candidate draws minimal attention and resources.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.4211, "margin": -1.21}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.4651, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.3497, "margin": -3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.3248, "margin": -3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.2949, "margin": -3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.3493, "margin": -2.0}, {"date": "2026-